.\" Manpage for elisp (elisprs).
.\" Contact https://github.com/MenkeTechnologies/elisprs to correct errors or omissions.
.TH ELISP 1 "2026-10-03" "elisprs 0.1.19" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
elisp \- Emacs Lisp interpreter (run .el outside Emacs; Lisp-2 obarray with lexical and dynamic binding, compiled to fusevm bytecode)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B elisp
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-e\fR \fIPROGRAM\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B elisp
evaluates Emacs Lisp as a standalone program. It is the command-line front
end of
.BR elisprs ,
an Emacs Lisp runtime in Rust built to become a frontend for the
.B fusevm
bytecode VM (the engine behind
.BR zshrs ,
.BR stryke ,
.BR awkrs ,
and
.BR vimlrs ).
.PP
Emacs Lisp is a Lisp-2 \(em every symbol carries a separate value cell and
function cell \(em with dynamic binding by default and lexical binding under a
\fBlexical\-binding\fR cookie. The reader, the object heap, the obarray and the
standard library are elisprs's own. Each top-level form is read, macroexpanded
and lowered to a \fBfusevm\fR chunk (\fIsrc/compiler.rs\fR), which fusevm runs,
calling back into the heap (\fIsrc/host.rs\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I FILE
Evaluate the forms in \fIFILE\fR (typically a \fB.el\fR file) in order.
.TP
.B \-\-script " " \fIFILE\fR
Evaluate \fIFILE\fR the way \fBemacs \-\-script\fR does: the forms run inside the
\fB\ *load*\fR buffer, so they read the standard syntax table. Without this
option \fBelisp\fR \fIFILE\fR models \fBemacs \-l\fR \fIFILE\fR, whose forms run in
\fB*scratch*\fR and read the Emacs Lisp mode table \(em the two disagree on, for
example, \fB(char\-syntax ?.)\fR (46 vs 95).
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-eval " " \fIPROGRAM\fR
Evaluate \fIPROGRAM\fR and print the value of the last form.
.TP
.B \-\-repl
Start the interactive reedline REPL: a utop-style line editor with Tab
completion over the special-form and subr wordlist, a live ASCII stats banner,
emacs/vi edit modes (\fBELISPRS_REPL_MODE\fR or \fI~/.elisprs/config.toml\fR),
and history at \fI~/.elisprs/history\fR. With no arguments on an interactive
terminal, \fBelisp\fR launches this REPL automatically; piped input falls back
to a plain line-buffered reader.
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Run the language server over stdio: completion, hover, signature help,
document symbols and reader diagnostics.
.TP
.B \-\-dap
Run the line-level debug adapter over stdio (breakpoints, stepping, variables).
.TP
.BR \-\-aot " " \fIFILE\fR " " \fB\-o\fR " " \fIOUT\fR
Ahead-of-time compile \fIFILE\fR to a native object \fIOUT\fR via
\fBfusevm::aot::compile_object\fR (the lowered chunk plus its heap image).
.TP
.BR \-\-dump\-tokens " " \fIFILE\fR
Print the reader's lexical token stream for \fIFILE\fR (one \fBline  Tok\fR per
line) and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-dump\-ast " " \fIFILE\fR
Print each top-level form of \fIFILE\fR as its read s-expression (the elisp
AST), one form per line, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-dump\-bytecode " " \fIFILE\fR
Print the compiled \fBfusevm\fR ops for \fIFILE\fR and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-tiers
Run it, then report which fusevm execution tier took each of its chunks.
.TP
.BR \-\-disasm " " \fIFILE\fR
Print a \fBfusevm\fR bytecode disassembly of \fIFILE\fR (via the shared
\fBfusevm::Chunk::disassemble\fR) and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B elisp
starts a read-eval-print loop. Input is accumulated until parentheses balance,
so multi-line forms work; Ctrl-D exits.
.SH LANGUAGE
Reader syntax: integers, floats, strings with escapes, symbols (including
\fB1+\fR / \fB1\-\fR / \fB<=\fR / \fB:keywords\fR), \fBnil\fR / \fBt\fR,
\fB'\fRquote, \fB#'\fRfunction, \fB?c\fR character literals, \fB;\fR comments,
dotted pairs, vectors, and backquote: \fB`\fR, \fB,\fR and \fB,@\fR read as
\fB(\e` X)\fR, \fB(\e, X)\fR and \fB(\e,@ X)\fR, as in Emacs, and the \fB`\fR
macro expands them.
.PP
Special forms: \fBquote function lambda progn prog1 if when unless cond and or
while setq let let* defun defmacro defvar defconst condition-case
unwind-protect\fR.
Rust subrs and a preloaded elisp prelude cover lists, arithmetic, strings,
buffers, regexps, printing, cl\-lib, pcase, seq and subr\-x.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Known divergences from GNU Emacs, and the ones still open, are recorded in
\fIBUGS.md\fR in the source tree. There is no display: buffers, point and
markers exist, windows and frames do not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B elisp
exits 0 on success and non-zero if a file cannot be read or evaluation signals
an uncaught error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Evaluate an expression:
.PP
.RS
.nf
elisp \-e '(mapcar #'\(aq1+ \(aq(1 2 3))'   \(rh (2 3 4)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
elisp examples/demo.el
.fi
.RE
